# 🔴 This is an ALLOW-LIST, not a deny-list. Everything is ignored by default and
#    only the file types this project actually ships are let back in.
#
#    Why it is inverted: this repo is developed inside an agent harness, so private
#    files (agent workspace, local config, screenshots, session state) live in the
#    same tree as the published source. A deny-list only blocks what someone thought
#    to list. Measured 2026-08-24: a deny-list that looked complete still let through
#    .env, .claude/, scratchpad/, job files, `shot.png` from `wb shot`, and a Chrome
#    `Login Data` DB. The failure mode is silent — you find out after it is public.
#
#    A deny-list is wrong here for a structural reason: a new kind of junk file is
#    ignored by default, while a new source file fails loudly at `git status` and
#    gets a line added below. Mistakes land on the safe side.
#
# To add a genuinely new source file type, add an un-ignore line in the right group.

# ---- deny everything -------------------------------------------------------
/*
!/.gitignore

# ---- directories this project ships ----------------------------------------
!/bin/
!/docs/
!/jobs/
!/scripts/
!/skills/
!/test/
!/bench/
!/.github/

# 🔴 An allow-listed directory opens *everything* under it, including build
#    droppings. `__pycache__` reached the very first commit of this repo that
#    way and nobody spotted it. Deny these again, after the allow rules.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ---- source ----------------------------------------------------------------
!*.js
!*.py
!*.sh
!*.html
!/wb
!/wbrowser.service

# ---- documentation and metadata --------------------------------------------
!*.md
!/LICENSE
!/package.json
!/server.json
!/accounts.json
!/.github/**/*.yml

# ---- job definitions: examples only ----------------------------------------
# 🔴 A job file names the sites you automate and the selectors you click.
#    That is your routine, so the default is "not published".
/jobs/*
!/jobs/example-*.json

# ---- re-deny things that would otherwise slip through the rules above ------
# These matter because the extension rules are broad: a stray note.md or a
# harness CLAUDE.md would otherwise be let straight back in by `!*.md`.
/CLAUDE.md
/project-specific.md
/AGENT/
/.claude/
/.handoff/
/.mcp.json
/scratchpad/
/tmp/
/node_modules/
/package-lock.json

# Runtime state — a cookie IS the login
/.wbrowser*/
*session.json
*runtime.json
*Cookies*
*Login Data*

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
